The pointer is no longer held inside a game's window in Mission Control, and the hidden cursor is set again after it.

This repository's patch to the Mac driver, to be offered to WineHQ (it needs nothing from patches 0013 and 0014,
but its last line uses 0014's cursorNeedsReapply; without 0014 that line is left out).

The fault: with a game in front that clips the cursor (ClipCursor; DirectInput's exclusive mouse clips to the
window), opening Mission Control kept the pointer inside the game's window area, so no other window could be
clicked; and back in the game, the Mac arrow showed over it until a Cmd-Tab out and back (mavrik S212, Skyrim SE
on macOS 27.0.1).

Why: the Mac driver's default clipping (WineConfinementClipCursorHandler, the undocumented
-[NSWindow setMouseConfinementRect:]) is kept by the window server while the app is frontmost and the window is
key. Mission Control and App Exposé belong to the Dock, and the app stays active through them, so the confinement
stays. The older event tap handler already lets events destined for another process through unclipped ("This may
happen if the user activates Exposé or Mission Control"), but the confinement handler has no such case. The app
gets no event while the Dock has the mouse, so nothing told the driver to set its hidden cursor again either.

The fix:
- While the app is active, a global NSEvent monitor watches mouse moves, drags and presses. A global monitor sees
  only events that go to other processes, so it does nothing in normal play; it is removed when the app resigns
  active. Mouse events need no permission for it (unlike key events).
- Such an event lifts the confinement rect (the clip rect and the Windows side's clipping state are kept), makes
  the next mouse move absolute, and marks the cursor for setting again.
- The next mouse move over a Wine window puts the confinement back for the same clip rect, moves the pointer into
  it as starting a clip does, and the cursor is set again (0014's re-apply).
- The event tap handler is unchanged; it already handles this case in its tap.

Checked (mavrik S213): applies to the CrossOver 26.3.0 tree after patches 0001 to 0014 with no fuzz; cocoa_app.m and
cocoa_cursorclipping.m compile with the tree's warning flags with no new warning; on macOS 27.0.1
setMouseConfinementRect: is present (so it is the route games take) and a global mouse monitor is made with no
prompt. Not yet checked live: Mission Control itself, which needs the screen (mavrik's r7 cursor check).

